1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,360
On April 16, 1991, Bernie Madoff
said he bought 123 call options on

2
00:00:06,380 --> 00:00:08,070
Procter & Gamble stock.

3
00:00:08,280 --> 00:00:09,290
One problem.

4
00:00:09,380 --> 00:00:12,060
In the entire market, that whole day,

5
00:00:12,080 --> 00:00:14,810
only 20 of those options changed hands.

6
00:00:15,060 --> 00:00:17,820
He claimed six times more than existed,

7
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:21,190
and nobody stopped him for 17 more years.

8
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:25,930
By the time it collapsed, the
fake balances said $65 billion.

9
00:00:27,150 --> 00:00:27,860
Here's the thing.

10
00:00:27,880 --> 00:00:31,070
That impossible number isn't a fluke.

11
00:00:31,120 --> 00:00:33,840
The biggest frauds in
history all hide one.

12
00:00:33,860 --> 00:00:36,070
A number that cannot exist.

13
00:00:36,520 --> 00:00:37,940
Today, three of them.

14
00:00:37,960 --> 00:00:40,190
A coupon scam from 1920.

15
00:00:40,280 --> 00:00:42,320
A tank farm full of water.

16
00:00:42,340 --> 00:00:44,850
And the fund that almost never lost.

17
00:00:45,100 --> 00:00:47,380
And by the end, you'll
have a three-question test

18
00:00:47,400 --> 00:00:49,140
that would have caught all three.

19
00:00:49,160 --> 00:00:52,570
Because the next one won't
call itself a Ponzi scheme.

20
00:00:53,780 --> 00:00:54,650
Chapter 1.

21
00:00:54,840 --> 00:00:59,350
The Coupon That Couldn't
Exist Boston, 1920.

22
00:01:00,280 --> 00:01:02,750
Banks are paying about 5% a year.

23
00:01:02,900 --> 00:01:08,990
And a former dishwasher named Charles
Ponzi is promising 50% in 45 days.

24
00:01:09,280 --> 00:01:10,950
His pitch sounded clever.

25
00:01:11,260 --> 00:01:13,310
International reply coupons.

26
00:01:13,540 --> 00:01:15,740
Little slips you could
buy cheap in Europe, where

27
00:01:15,760 --> 00:01:18,540
money was weak after the
war, and trade in America

28
00:01:18,560 --> 00:01:20,390
for stamps worth more.

29
00:01:20,540 --> 00:01:23,190
Buy low there, cash in high here.

30
00:01:23,380 --> 00:01:25,420
On paper, that's arbitrage.

31
00:01:25,440 --> 00:01:26,550
It's even legal.

32
00:01:26,640 --> 00:01:29,090
And for a while, it looked like it worked.

33
00:01:29,460 --> 00:01:31,020
Early investors got paid.

34
00:01:31,040 --> 00:01:32,010
On time.

35
00:01:32,240 --> 00:01:33,250
Word spread.

36
00:01:33,420 --> 00:01:37,830
By July, money was pouring in at
close to a million dollars a day.

37
00:01:37,880 --> 00:01:40,570
Even most of Boston's
police force bought in.

38
00:01:40,700 --> 00:01:42,490
But nobody did the math.

39
00:01:42,740 --> 00:01:46,070
Until a financial journalist
named Clarence Barron did.

40
00:01:46,180 --> 00:01:51,060
To cover what Ponzi owed, 160 million
postal coupons would have to be in

41
00:01:51,080 --> 00:01:52,010
circulation.

42
00:01:52,220 --> 00:01:53,430
The actual number?

43
00:01:53,540 --> 00:01:55,530
About 27,000.

44
00:01:55,640 --> 00:01:57,400
That's not a little short.

45
00:01:57,420 --> 00:02:00,870
That's about one coupon
for every 6,000 he'd need.

46
00:02:01,100 --> 00:02:03,110
So where were the profits coming from?

47
00:02:03,380 --> 00:02:04,690
The next investor.

48
00:02:05,080 --> 00:02:07,850
Old investors were paid
with new investors' money.

49
00:02:07,920 --> 00:02:09,190
That's the whole trick.

50
00:02:09,340 --> 00:02:11,800
When the papers ran the
numbers, the line outside his

51
00:02:11,820 --> 00:02:13,710
office turned into a run.

52
00:02:14,020 --> 00:02:15,630
Six banks went down.

53
00:02:16,040 --> 00:02:18,470
Investors lost about 20 million dollars.

54
00:02:18,520 --> 00:02:22,400
In 1920, Ponzi pleaded
guilty to mail fraud.

55
00:02:22,420 --> 00:02:24,240
His name became the word for the scam.

56
00:02:24,260 --> 00:02:28,590
And he died broke, with
an estate of 75 dollars.

57
00:02:29,420 --> 00:02:32,690
Ponzi's impossible number
was hiding in coupons.

58
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:35,440
The next one was hiding in plain sight,

59
00:02:35,460 --> 00:02:36,750
inside a tank.

60
00:02:37,760 --> 00:02:38,670
Chapter 2.

61
00:02:39,080 --> 00:02:40,750
More oil than America.

62
00:02:41,120 --> 00:02:42,040
New Jersey.

63
00:02:42,060 --> 00:02:44,150
The early 1960s.

64
00:02:44,500 --> 00:02:48,760
Tino De Angelis runs a vegetable
oil company called Allied Crude, out

65
00:02:48,780 --> 00:02:51,110
of a run-down tank farm in Bayonne.

66
00:02:51,240 --> 00:02:53,930
His business ran on a simple kind of loan.

67
00:02:54,040 --> 00:02:55,870
Oil sits in a tank.

68
00:02:56,080 --> 00:02:59,130
A warehouse company checks
the tank and writes a receipt.

69
00:02:59,680 --> 00:03:01,800
Banks lend money against the receipt.

70
00:03:01,820 --> 00:03:03,650
And the company writing those receipts?

71
00:03:03,920 --> 00:03:05,870
A unit of American Express.

72
00:03:06,320 --> 00:03:08,700
If American Express vouched for the oil,

73
00:03:08,720 --> 00:03:09,810
the banks paid.

74
00:03:10,140 --> 00:03:12,250
So De Angelis made more oil.

75
00:03:12,380 --> 00:03:13,690
Not real oil.

76
00:03:13,780 --> 00:03:14,810
Receipt oil.

77
00:03:14,940 --> 00:03:19,190
Some tanks had hidden compartments,
so inspectors saw oil at the hatch.

78
00:03:19,600 --> 00:03:22,210
Others were mostly water,
because oil floats.

79
00:03:22,400 --> 00:03:25,040
Inspectors looked at the top, saw oil,

80
00:03:25,060 --> 00:03:25,910
and signed.

81
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:27,860
And when they walked to the next tank,

82
00:03:27,880 --> 00:03:30,400
pipes moved the same oil ahead of them.

83
00:03:30,420 --> 00:03:33,770
The same oil, counted again and again.

84
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:35,810
Here's the impossible number.

85
00:03:35,960 --> 00:03:40,220
By 1963, Allied claimed more
soybean and cottonseed oil

86
00:03:40,240 --> 00:03:43,290
than existed in the entire United States.

87
00:03:43,400 --> 00:03:46,330
On paper, 900,000 tons.

88
00:03:46,580 --> 00:03:49,140
Actually there, about 55,000.

89
00:03:49,160 --> 00:03:52,530
Over 90% of it never existed.

90
00:03:53,060 --> 00:03:55,710
Then he tried to corner
the market in oil futures.

91
00:03:56,060 --> 00:03:58,750
When prices cracked in November 1963,

92
00:03:58,820 --> 00:04:02,950
the margin calls came, and somebody
finally looked inside the tanks.

93
00:04:03,780 --> 00:04:04,650
51 lenders.

94
00:04:04,860 --> 00:04:07,990
More than $180 million in losses.

95
00:04:08,340 --> 00:04:11,080
The American Express
warehouse unit went bankrupt.

96
00:04:11,100 --> 00:04:13,190
A Wall Street brokerage went under.

97
00:04:13,300 --> 00:04:16,730
De Angelis pleaded guilty and
was sentenced to 20 years.

98
00:04:16,920 --> 00:04:18,510
He was out in seven.

99
00:04:18,740 --> 00:04:19,830
Coupons.

100
00:04:19,920 --> 00:04:20,550
Tanks.

101
00:04:20,960 --> 00:04:24,710
Both frauds needed more of something
than the world actually had.

102
00:04:24,800 --> 00:04:28,400
But the biggest one in history
needed something even rarer.

103
00:04:28,420 --> 00:04:30,030
A perfect record.

104
00:04:30,880 --> 00:04:31,950
Chapter 3.

105
00:04:32,180 --> 00:04:37,330
The Fund That Almost Never Lost
Bernie Madoff wasn't a nobody.

106
00:04:37,520 --> 00:04:39,450
He'd been chairman of the NASDAQ.

107
00:04:39,560 --> 00:04:43,290
His trading firm was one of the
biggest market makers on Wall Street.

108
00:04:43,400 --> 00:04:47,120
And his investment fund had a
pitch that sounded almost boring.

109
00:04:47,140 --> 00:04:50,310
No 50%, just steady returns.

110
00:04:50,380 --> 00:04:51,990
Around 10% a year.

111
00:04:52,120 --> 00:04:54,670
Year after year after year.

112
00:04:54,980 --> 00:04:56,770
That was the genius of it.

113
00:04:56,940 --> 00:04:58,450
Ponzi sold greed.

114
00:04:58,820 --> 00:05:00,490
Madoff sold safety.

115
00:05:00,800 --> 00:05:05,350
One fund reported 10.5%
a year for 17 years.

116
00:05:05,580 --> 00:05:08,340
In late 2008, while markets were crashing,

117
00:05:08,360 --> 00:05:10,830
it said it was still up for the year.

118
00:05:11,100 --> 00:05:13,420
Now picture a real investment chart.

119
00:05:13,440 --> 00:05:14,380
It wiggles.

120
00:05:14,400 --> 00:05:14,840
It drops.

121
00:05:14,860 --> 00:05:17,710
It has bad months and bad years.

122
00:05:18,060 --> 00:05:22,550
Madoff's line went up at an
almost perfect 45 degree angle.

123
00:05:22,820 --> 00:05:25,640
A financial analyst named
Harry Markopolos was asked

124
00:05:25,660 --> 00:05:27,550
to copy Madoff's strategy.

125
00:05:27,620 --> 00:05:30,180
He later said he knew
within five minutes that

126
00:05:30,200 --> 00:05:31,740
the numbers didn't add up.

127
00:05:31,760 --> 00:05:34,280
It took him four hours to prove it.

128
00:05:34,300 --> 00:05:36,250
His red flags were math.

129
00:05:36,560 --> 00:05:40,570
Madoff reported losing months
only about 4% of the time.

130
00:05:40,660 --> 00:05:42,240
Real markets don't move like that.

131
00:05:42,260 --> 00:05:45,170
And the strategy itself was impossible.

132
00:05:45,440 --> 00:05:47,360
To make the trades Madoff claimed,

133
00:05:47,380 --> 00:05:50,520
he would have needed more options on
the Chicago Board Options Exchange

134
00:05:50,540 --> 00:05:52,410
than actually existed.

135
00:05:52,560 --> 00:05:54,430
Same pattern as 1991.

136
00:05:55,320 --> 00:05:58,310
123 options claimed, 20 traded.

137
00:05:58,540 --> 00:06:01,830
Markopolos took his
math to the SEC in 2000.

138
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:02,890
And again.

139
00:06:03,020 --> 00:06:06,940
In 2005, he sent a memo titled,
The world's largest hedge

140
00:06:06,960 --> 00:06:08,530
fund is a fraud.

141
00:06:08,700 --> 00:06:13,630
Over 16 years, regulators looked at
Madoff's firm at least eight times.

142
00:06:13,680 --> 00:06:15,250
They didn't find the fraud.

143
00:06:15,620 --> 00:06:17,710
Then, 2008 happened.

144
00:06:18,100 --> 00:06:21,020
Markets crashed and investors
asked for their money back.

145
00:06:21,040 --> 00:06:23,540
About $7 billion of it.

146
00:06:23,560 --> 00:06:27,600
Madoff's bank account, which had held
over $5 billion earlier that year,

147
00:06:27,620 --> 00:06:30,650
was down to about $234 million.

148
00:06:30,840 --> 00:06:34,420
On December 10, he told his sons
he was finished and that the

149
00:06:34,440 --> 00:06:36,250
fund was a Ponzi scheme.

150
00:06:36,380 --> 00:06:37,890
They went to the authorities.

151
00:06:38,100 --> 00:06:39,900
The next morning, he was arrested.

152
00:06:39,920 --> 00:06:46,030
Prosecutors put the fraud at $64.8
billion based on the fake statements.

153
00:06:46,220 --> 00:06:51,000
Markopolos estimated that 35 to 55
billion of that was profit that never

154
00:06:51,020 --> 00:06:52,630
existed at all.

155
00:06:52,800 --> 00:06:55,660
Madoff pleaded guilty to
11 federal crimes and was

156
00:06:55,680 --> 00:06:58,330
sentenced to 150 years.

157
00:06:58,440 --> 00:07:00,710
He died in prison in 2021.

158
00:07:01,740 --> 00:07:04,050
So, here's the impossible number test.

159
00:07:04,160 --> 00:07:05,190
Three questions.

160
00:07:05,340 --> 00:07:07,150
They would have caught all three.

161
00:07:07,330 --> 00:07:08,490
Question one.

162
00:07:08,620 --> 00:07:11,370
Does the math need more
of something than exists?

163
00:07:11,680 --> 00:07:12,690
More coupons.

164
00:07:12,820 --> 00:07:13,850
More oil.

165
00:07:13,940 --> 00:07:15,090
More options.

166
00:07:15,400 --> 00:07:18,120
If a return needs a market
bigger than the market,

167
00:07:18,140 --> 00:07:19,410
it's not a return.

168
00:07:19,760 --> 00:07:20,770
Question two.

169
00:07:20,940 --> 00:07:22,350
Is it too smooth?

170
00:07:22,400 --> 00:07:24,050
Real money wiggles.

171
00:07:24,160 --> 00:07:27,340
If something never has a
bad month, that's not skill.

172
00:07:27,360 --> 00:07:28,510
That's a story.

173
00:07:28,800 --> 00:07:29,890
Question three.

174
00:07:29,960 --> 00:07:32,030
Can anyone check it besides them?

175
00:07:32,140 --> 00:07:34,080
Ponzi kept his books on index cards.

176
00:07:34,100 --> 00:07:37,650
De Angelis's inspectors
had close ties to him.

177
00:07:37,740 --> 00:07:41,110
Madoff ran his own brokerage,
so he checked his own homework.

178
00:07:41,540 --> 00:07:42,440
Three frauds.

179
00:07:42,460 --> 00:07:43,910
Three different eras.

180
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:44,960
One pattern.

181
00:07:44,980 --> 00:07:47,090
A number that can't exist.

182
00:07:47,380 --> 00:07:49,980
So, next time someone
shows you a perfect chart,

183
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:51,700
don't ask how much you could make.

184
00:07:51,720 --> 00:07:53,950
Ask what it would take for it to be true.

185
00:07:54,600 --> 00:07:57,220
Subscribe for the next case and
tell me in the comments which red

186
00:07:57,240 --> 00:07:59,270
flag would have caught you first.

187
00:07:59,380 --> 00:08:02,390
Then, watch the tank that
fooled American Express.

